WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:03.180
ROSE: Welcome, everybody.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.319
My name is Gideon Rose.

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:08.980
I am the editor of Foreign Affairs, and I'm
delighted to be able to welcome you to another

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:14.830
Foreign Affairs Live, this one on new emerging
markets, which is based on our package in

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.970
the Jan-Feb issue, "Where to Bet Now."

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.370
Let me just say a little word about this,
because some people might say, gee, what is

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.060
Foreign Affairs doing talking about emerging
markets?

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.390
And this is not your kind of wheelhouse.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.089
And why are you in there at all?

00:00:30.089 --> 00:00:37.760
And for 90 years, we've been purveying a steady
stream of serious expert commentary on a whole

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.760
range of subjects.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:45.300
Our best known subject matter has always been,
of course, U.S. foreign policy and grand strategy

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.989
and security studies and diplomacy, but we've
done a lot of other things, as well, and certainly

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:53.890
the original vision was one that essentially
covered the world.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:59.800
And in today's world, our feeling is that,
if it's important, we should be covering it,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.500
and we should be covering it with the same
kind of seriousness intellectually, the same

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.950
kind of practical relevance, and the same
kind of accessible presentation that we've

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.060
always brought to other kinds of things.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.081
And so whether it's emerging markets, whether
it's technology, which is the subject of the

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:19.600
lead package in our March-April issue, whether
it's energy markets, which are the subject

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.400
of our -- the lead package in May-June, we're
going to be doing not just all the things

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.030
we used to do and better than ever, but we're
going to be tackling whatever else is out

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:33.350
there that is important, the trends that are
driving the world that are important to our

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:38.270
clientele, and bringing to it the same kind
of expertise and accessibility and authority

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.410
that we've brought to the things that we're
traditionally better known for.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.960
So that's, if you're ever wondering why we're
doing this kind of thing, expect more of the

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.770
-- expect more of this.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:53.040
But it's not so much a new departure, as it
is an expansion of the franchise to all things

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.400
that matter.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:03.600
So with that, everybody knows that, despite
the recent week or so in which the public

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.720
seemed to pick up on it for the first time,
or in a big way, the major emerging markets

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.920
that have been hot over the last several years
have started to come undone.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.720
The BRICs are crumbling.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.890
Some of the major countries that were doing
very well a few years ago, you could throw

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:24.320
a dart -- you know, throw a dart at a dartboard
and do well by investing in the developing

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.530
world, that's not true now.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.110
That said, there are some green shoots out
there.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.690
There are some very promising stories.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:40.860
And if you look at a stagnating Europe, a
slowing China, a muddling forward in a positive,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.930
but not particularly exciting way U.S., we
thought that it was very important that -- and

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.870
worth time -- to essentially take a look at
some of the newer emerging markets, the hotter

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:57.210
developing countries that people hadn't focused
on as much, and say, gee, where are places

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:02.060
that over the next several years are going
to be interesting to watch and worthwhile

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.160
to -- to maybe bet on.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.319
And what we did was we went out and found
sort of absolutely top experts in each of

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:14.860
those countries to present to us honestly
and straightforwardly, not in a kind of touting

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.770
way, not in a national champion kind of way,
just what are the secrets of this country's

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.990
success to date?

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:25.690
What are the reasons to be optimistic about
it in the world that's going forward?

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.700
And what are the challenges that have to be
overcome?

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.840
What are the walls of worry that have to be
climbed?

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.240
Because, of course, every place has all sorts
of problems that need to be overcome.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.530
This is our attempt to basically dip into
sort of global economic forecasting.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:44.410
And without further ado, let me introduce
our panel, and we'll get right to it.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:49.760
So, Marcus Noland is the executive vice president
and director of studies at the Peterson Institute

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.790
for International Economics, and he's going
to talk about South Korea.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.290
Mitchell Orenstein is the chair of the political
science department at Northeastern University,

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.670
and he's going to talk to us about Poland.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:07.460
And Steve Cook is the Hasib Sabbagh Senior
Fellow for Middle Eastern Studies at the Council.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.160
He's going to talk to us about Turkey.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:14.250
David Dombey -- sorry, yeah, Dombey wrote
the piece in the package on Turkey.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.671
He couldn't be with us tonight, but Steve
is not only a good friend, has written for

00:04:17.671 --> 00:04:21.239
us on Turkey many times, and is going to be
the Turkey expert tonight.

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:23.400
I urge you to look at the whole package...

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.740
COOK: And Dombey's taller than me.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:29.250
ROSE: ... to look at the whole -- whole package,
and Mexico is obviously one of the most interesting

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.630
countries, and Shannon's piece on that is
quite good.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:38.870
And the two pieces on ASEAN, sort of the archipelagoes
and the mainland one, highlight countries

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:43.140
that people haven't really paid all that much
attention to outside pure regionalists and

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.280
are also worth a close look.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.030
Without further ado, so, Marcus, why is Korea
in this?

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.100
And why are we -- what are the reasons to
be excited and/or worried about Korea?

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.310
NOLAND: Well, I think the reason that Korea
is in it tonight is that Korea is the premier

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.350
development success story of the last 50 years.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:09.300
Fifty years ago, South Korea was poorer than
Mozambique or Bolivia or Ghana.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.550
And today, it's richer than Spain and New
Zealand.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:22.780
It was the first non-OECD -- excuse me, non-G-7
or -G-8 country to host the G-20 summit.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:28.220
It was the first Asian country to host the
G-20 summit.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:35.490
But as remarkable as its economic transformation
has been, its political transformation was

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.100
arguably even more stunning.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:43.110
In a period of a decade, from the late 1980s
to the late 1990s, they went from a military

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:50.860
dictator to his hand-picked general who was
elected successor to an elected civilian centrist

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:56.940
politician to electing a political dissident
who a previous leader had tried to kill as

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.850
president of the country.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:05.430
And it was a country that hosted the second
nuclear summit and now a Korean national former

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.520
foreign minister is secretary general of the
United Nations.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.210
So it is a country that has had stunning success
in economics, has had success in politics,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:20.380
and those successes are now being recognized
at the international level.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:25.430
When Jonathan Tepperman first approached me
about participating in this project, I actually

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.820
told him I wasn't really sure, you know, I
should do it, because in some ways, the talk

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.030
about Korea as an emerging market is a bit
of an anachronism.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:37.740
Korea today has a per capita income of $25,000.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.670
It's a trillion-dollar economy.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:43.150
Now, it is a very open economy.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.130
Exports plus imports exceed national income.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.480
Trade ratio is more than 100 percent.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.550
And that means that South Korea is unusually
vulnerable to shocks.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:59.550
And in that sense, it is a riskier economy
in which to invest than, say, the United States

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.190
or Japan or Canada.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:07.340
So in that sense, there is this kind of aspect
that it is -- it has not completely made the

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.540
transition into a kind of emerged economy.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.650
There is still an emerging economy aspect
to it.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:19.190
That said, in the last couple weeks, when
investor concerns have really been roiling

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:26.260
markets, the impact on South Korea has been
nothing, for example, compared to the impact

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:27.390
on Turkey.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:32.919
So it seems like investors are really putting
it in a different basket.

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:37.389
Challenges moving forward is that growth is
slowing.

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:43.300
For the first time in history, trend growth
is probably something on the order of 3 percent

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.820
to 3.5 percent, which actually means it's
growing slower than the world average for

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.730
the first time.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:55.669
And South Koreans put a lot of demands on
their government, so the government has recently

00:07:55.669 --> 00:08:01.300
begun -- this month announced that they're
going to have a three-year plan.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.650
They've established some very ambitious goals.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:07.900
They want to have per capita income get from
$25,000 to $30,000.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.670
They want to raise the rate of growth to 40
percent.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:15.880
They want to raise the labor participation
rate to 70 percent, which would imply substantial

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.240
increases in labor participation by women
and young people.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.150
So it's a very ambitious set of targets.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.009
They haven't actually released the plan.

00:08:24.009 --> 00:08:27.180
The plan is supposed to come out this coming
month.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.139
But they've identified three strategies that
they want to use.

00:08:32.139 --> 00:08:37.819
The first is, there's an emphasis on building
a more competitive economy.

00:08:37.819 --> 00:08:40.539
And what exactly does that mean?

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:46.171
South Korea's been really successful economically,
but part of that success has been that it

00:08:46.171 --> 00:08:49.329
is a kind of unbalanced economy.

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:55.379
Very, very large conglomerates account for
a lot of the economy.

00:08:55.379 --> 00:08:59.380
And while the manufacturing sector has obviously
done very well -- everybody in this room is

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:07.959
familiar with Samsung and Hyundai -- the service
sector has really lagged and is highly regulated.

00:09:07.959 --> 00:09:14.529
And in particular, there's a whole plethora
of state-owned enterprises or government-sponsored

00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:19.500
enterprises or parastatals -- I'm not exactly
sure how you'd describe them all -- but there

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:24.149
is a sense that they can operate not exactly
conforming to market rules.

00:09:24.149 --> 00:09:28.790
And, in fact, there's a lot of concern about
the debts that these organizations are running

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:29.790
up.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:34.490
So the first part of the plan is try to level
out the playing field to have a more competitive

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:35.820
economy.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.809
Second is an emphasis on creativity.

00:09:39.809 --> 00:09:46.439
I suppose everybody in this room is familiar
with, you know, "Gangnam Style" and, you know,

00:09:46.439 --> 00:09:49.889
the whole boom in South Korean cinema.

00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:56.389
And so that's kind of a code word for shifting
away from manufacturing and towards services.

00:09:56.389 --> 00:10:00.390
How you do that is not so clear.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:07.649
And, finally, there's an emphasis on rebalancing
away from external demand to domestic demand.

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:11.639
But, again, if you actually listen to what
President Park Geun-hye has been saying over

00:10:11.639 --> 00:10:17.709
the last month, she's actually been talking
about really ways of milking external demand

00:10:17.709 --> 00:10:22.861
a little more, not such clear ideas on how
to shift towards domestic demand, although

00:10:22.861 --> 00:10:27.290
the National Assembly is bringing forward
some expenditures.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:28.430
Final thing.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.529
So far, everything I've said is pretty conventional.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:33.860
I mean, the Korean story is outstanding, of
course.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:36.339
But it's all kind of comprehensible.

00:10:36.339 --> 00:10:41.950
There is one thing that sets South Korea apart,
that is the world's largest contingent liability,

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.209
North Korea.

00:10:44.209 --> 00:10:49.920
And one of the really curious things in the
last month, not only in South Korea, but then

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.199
at Davos, President Park has been talking
about unification.

00:10:55.199 --> 00:10:58.910
And she's been talking about unification in
very positive terms.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:02.540
She's described it as a jackpot, as a bonanza.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:07.989
Now, personally, I think there are many reasons
to look forward to Korean unification.

00:11:07.989 --> 00:11:13.029
I mean, I look forward to the disappearance
of the current North Korean regime.

00:11:13.029 --> 00:11:19.309
Stimulus to the South Korean economy is not
high on my list of reasons about why I am

00:11:19.309 --> 00:11:20.809
looking forward to that.

00:11:20.809 --> 00:11:26.110
But between interesting developments in the
economy and potentially enormous developments

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:32.649
with respect to North Korea, I think South
Korea remains a very interesting and exciting

00:11:32.649 --> 00:11:33.649
country.

00:11:33.649 --> 00:11:34.769
ROSE: OK.

00:11:34.769 --> 00:11:40.230
My son is 13 and is having his bar mitzvah
in two weeks, and you can tell that Korea

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:48.230
has come of age because Psy, the "Gangnam
Style" pop star, is actually on the New York

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.239
bar mitzvah circuit.

00:11:49.239 --> 00:11:50.920
And you can check on Instagram.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:51.980
He is available.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:56.720
You can have Psy do "Gangnam Style" at your
child's bar mitzvah, if you're prepared to

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:02.970
pony up, which is something that I don't think
was ever the case in previous kinds of things.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:03.990
True story.

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:10.749
Mitch, you know, 20 years ago, the idea that
Poland would be featured at a panel like this

00:12:10.749 --> 00:12:12.120
would have been laughable.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.240
And 70 years ago, there wasn't a Poland.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:21.129
So explain how Poland went -- as in the title
of your piece -- from tragedy to triumph?

00:12:21.129 --> 00:12:23.439
ORENSTEIN: Yeah, well, thank you so much.

00:12:23.439 --> 00:12:28.049
I wanted to -- the previous speaker, Marcus
Noland, drew a kind of interesting contrast

00:12:28.049 --> 00:12:30.559
between South Korea and North Korea.

00:12:30.559 --> 00:12:34.709
And as I was writing the piece, I thought
about Foreign Affairs' trajectory that you

00:12:34.709 --> 00:12:39.429
mentioned and the way that it tries to encompass
-- I mean, its tradition is geopolitics, and

00:12:39.429 --> 00:12:44.120
trying to bring that geopolitical angle into
thinking about the economy.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:47.619
And so I was thinking, well, what are some
interesting contrasts you could draw with

00:12:47.619 --> 00:12:48.619
Poland?

00:12:48.619 --> 00:12:52.029
And I was thinking, well, you know, maybe
you could compare Poland to South Korea and

00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:55.540
have Belarus as their sort of North Korea,
you know?

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.269
I decided not to go that route...

00:12:58.269 --> 00:13:00.209
ROSE: Good choice.

00:13:00.209 --> 00:13:03.839
ORENSTEIN: ... but -- but I will bring up
another contrast that's extremely relevant

00:13:03.839 --> 00:13:05.389
today, which is Ukraine.

00:13:05.389 --> 00:13:08.429
As we look at Ukraine -- and I know a lot
of us in this room are following the events

00:13:08.429 --> 00:13:14.660
in Ukraine -- one's looking at the past of
European politics, that one's looking at a

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:21.269
country riven by serious geopolitical fault
lines, one in which the politics are extremely

00:13:21.269 --> 00:13:28.049
tenuous as a result, one in which the economic
progress has, therefore, been extremely muted

00:13:28.049 --> 00:13:30.160
in comparison to Poland.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.799
So when people ask me about my piece, which
is very bullish, very optimistic on Poland,

00:13:34.799 --> 00:13:36.949
and they say, why are you so optimistic about
Poland?

00:13:36.949 --> 00:13:39.209
I say, well, look at Ukraine.

00:13:39.209 --> 00:13:44.309
And indeed, this argument came up, as I understand,
in Davos recently.

00:13:44.309 --> 00:13:49.779
There were a couple tweets that I picked up
from Davos showing a nice chart of the trajectory

00:13:49.779 --> 00:13:56.290
of Poland's GDP growth, which has doubled
in proportion to E.U.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:00.410
average, whereas Ukraine has more or less
stagnated for the last 20 years.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:04.550
And the result is now that you have a GDP
in Poland which had been quite similar to

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:09.869
Ukraine historically is now about three times
on a per capita purchasing power parity basis.

00:14:09.869 --> 00:14:10.869
So...

00:14:10.869 --> 00:14:13.720
ROSE: Do the Poles make Ukrainian jokes?

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.529
NOLAND: They probably do, yeah.

00:14:15.529 --> 00:14:16.529
They probably do.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:21.040
Although I think today they have largely a
very hopeful outlook on Ukraine.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:28.279
So Poland is a country that was in that type
of Eastern European past, that had extreme

00:14:28.279 --> 00:14:29.279
political turmoil.

00:14:29.279 --> 00:14:32.769
It has an enormously tragic history.

00:14:32.769 --> 00:14:39.499
One can't step far in Poland without encountering
that sort of history on one side or another.

00:14:39.499 --> 00:14:44.199
And yet it's managed to overcome that in the
last 20 years to become one of the most successful

00:14:44.199 --> 00:14:45.509
economies in the world.

00:14:45.509 --> 00:14:49.279
It's now the 20th-largest economy -- or 24th-largest
economy in the world.

00:14:49.279 --> 00:14:52.259
It's on its way to being the 20th-largest.

00:14:52.259 --> 00:14:56.649
It's a country which has utterly changed its
geopolitical fate.

00:14:56.649 --> 00:14:58.189
It's no longer a Ukraine.

00:14:58.189 --> 00:15:01.269
It's a Poland.

00:15:01.269 --> 00:15:06.670
And it has really entered -- it's on the verge
of entering the core of the European Union.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:10.019
It will need to adopt the euro at some point
to make that happen.

00:15:10.019 --> 00:15:14.029
But it's a core -- it's becoming a core economy
of Europe, just as Britain is sort of moving

00:15:14.029 --> 00:15:17.829
out of that sort of status.

00:15:17.829 --> 00:15:20.179
So how did it do this?

00:15:20.179 --> 00:15:25.790
Well, I think part of the answer -- there's
a number of answers one could give to that,

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:31.320
but I think that one should go back to geopolitics
to really understand the core of it.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:36.869
One of the key answers I give in the piece
is that Poland has become a Western nation

00:15:36.869 --> 00:15:41.199
by virtue of the fact that its borders were,
in fact, moved at the end of the Second World

00:15:41.199 --> 00:15:42.199
War.

00:15:42.199 --> 00:15:46.230
I had a nice little chart to show this, but
I think a lot of people know the basic outlines.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:51.730
Poland existed several hundred miles to the
east up until 1945.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:56.329
It was an Eastern-facing country, a country
with an Eastern empire.

00:15:56.329 --> 00:16:00.730
In fact, when it got its nationhood in 1918,
practically the first thing it did is keep

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:05.489
marching further east and try to grab some
more territory away from the Soviet Union

00:16:05.489 --> 00:16:10.929
in 1920 to 1922, at the time of the civil
war in the Soviet Union.

00:16:10.929 --> 00:16:18.869
And after the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, which
took most of eastern Poland into the Soviet

00:16:18.869 --> 00:16:24.339
Union, Poland was compensated at the end of
the war with a whole lot of German territory

00:16:24.339 --> 00:16:25.980
which was seized.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:32.429
Those are the big areas of Pomerania, Silesia
and East Prussia.

00:16:32.429 --> 00:16:40.290
And so the map of Poland today is -- I want
to say -- more than a third former German

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:41.290
territory.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:45.309
And I have one line that I thought was going
to be controversial in the piece saying, you

00:16:45.309 --> 00:16:49.619
know, Poland today is, to a large extent Germany
inhabited by Poles.

00:16:49.619 --> 00:16:54.209
And that, of course, came about because of
dramatic expulsions, extremely devastating

00:16:54.209 --> 00:16:59.620
expulsions of people, where some millions
of Germans -- say, I think, 5 million or 6

00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:05.280
million Germans were expelled out of Poland
into Germany and 2 million or 3 million Poles

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:11.810
were expelled out of the Soviet -- the eastern
Polish territories, which are now Gud Lviv

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:17.880
(ph) and Vilnius, and move to the west, to
the emptied west of Poland.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:21.000
That for Stalin was a great move.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.740
It effectively punished the Germans after
the war.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:30.370
It expanded the territory of the Soviet Union
and in a way punished the Poles, also.

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:34.190
But what I don't think he looked at, at the
time, and what's become evident now, is that

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:36.570
it made Poland a Western nation.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:40.780
And where Poland had always vacillated, always
been a sort of marching ground for armies

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:46.980
in a sort of tug-of-war between Russia and
Germany, it effectively planted Poland in

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:52.540
this terrible history as firmly in the German
sphere of influence.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:55.550
And that's where it's come to at this moment.

00:17:55.550 --> 00:18:00.170
So the reason to invest in Poland, the reason
to look at Poland as a serious world economy

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:03.860
is because of its extremely tight relationship
with Germany.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:09.660
When you look at sort of the European story
-- you mentioned we talk a lot about European

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:14.880
stagnation, obviously the country that's not
stagnating and is actually doing quite well

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.600
in all of this is Germany.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:20.670
Germany -- the German economy is doing great.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:21.990
The strategy that...

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:22.990
(UNKNOWN): Testing.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:23.990
This isn't working.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:27.610
ORENSTEIN: ... they're pushing for the rest
of Europe is one that's really working for

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:28.610
Germany.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:29.990
And it's also working for Poland.

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:35.150
Poland, in contrast to other emerging markets,
has just had its growth outlook upgraded,

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:39.210
so it's now looking at 3 percent growth or
4 percent growth this year.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.210
Why?

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:48.010
Well, because Poland has become a -- has become
a low-cost platform, low-cost, high-quality

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:55.810
platform for production of European, but largely
German goods.

00:18:55.810 --> 00:19:01.830
This is really across-the-board, if you look
at any industry, but the primary one is the

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:11.780
auto industry, where companies like Volkswagen
employ thousands of workers in Poland, producing

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:20.280
all sorts of German automobiles, from trucks
to cars to luxury cars, not just cheap cars.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:25.030
Hugo Boss, the fashion-maker, makes a lot
of its products -- mainly its shoes -- in

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:26.400
Poland.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:30.360
MAN, the bus-maker, makes its stuff in Poland.

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:35.010
And now they're starting to employ nearly
as many workers in Poland as they are in Germany.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:43.159
In fact, one recent analysis by one of the
major investment banks showed that nearly

00:19:43.159 --> 00:19:49.590
-- I think was 30 percent or 40 percent of
Poland's exports to Germany end up as Germany's

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:53.010
exports to the rest of the world.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:59.650
So those German exports are including a huge
component of Poland.

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:04.320
So in this, it's interesting that Poland was
able to pull this off at a time when China

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:07.230
was rising in the global economy.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:14.130
And obviously, China became Eastern Europe,
and Poland in the foremost their main competitor.

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:19.290
You know, they were trying to attract capital
at the same time as Asia, Asian competitors,

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:21.950
like South Korea and China are taking off.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:25.450
And so they had to really prove that they
had a value proposition.

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:27.020
So what was that value proposition?

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:33.330
The value proposition was that you could make
goods cheaper in Poland than you could in

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:39.650
China, not every good, not very lightweight
goods, but heavy industry, things that have

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:47.360
substantial transport costs involved with
them, such as automotive, such as white goods,

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:54.600
where actually -- have become actually cheaper
to produce in Poland than to produce in China.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:21:00.180
And that's really, I think, the -- you know,
the sort of bottom line.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:05.131
In addition to that, of course, you have a
much more stable political environment by

00:21:05.131 --> 00:21:06.760
virtue of the E.U.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.700
You have the massive E.U.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:14.110
investment in Poland, which is building the
highways, the autobahn that Poland never had,

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:19.690
correcting a lot of their infrastructure problems,
tying them in much more closely to Western

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:24.930
Europe.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:29.240
And so you have the political thing and then,
of course, the proximity, which is that a

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:36.430
German manager can, you know, drive over to
the factory in Poland, take a look, walk around,

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:37.630
see it.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:45.240
So you have -- you have a whole system which
is much more monitorable and much more easily

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:49.950
compatible with European ways of doing things
than a factory in China.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:55.440
So I would say that Poland, for these reasons,
for its political stability, which is rooted

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:03.490
in the geopolitical situation, for the strong
linkages, the close ties, the way it's embraced

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:08.230
Germany is really an economy that's going
to be on the upswing for quite a long time,

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:14.320
and probably will do better over the next
10 or 20 years than the forecasts even think.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:16.860
ROSE: What are the challenges?

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.960
ORENSTEIN: Well, the challenges, I think,
are still political.

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:28.570
So some of you may -- some people will note
that the current government is on the verge

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:30.710
of losing its majority its parliament.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:34.160
It's facing elections where it's uncertain
to win.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:39.290
And as -- like a lot of countries in Europe
right now, the far-right or the more populist

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:41.620
right in Poland is rising.

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:47.520
And they typically have a more populist economic
platform, to some extent.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:50.630
They're more welfarist, in particular.

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:54.600
And people always raise questions about this
and wonder, you know, what's going to -- what

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.710
the effect is on the economy.

00:22:56.710 --> 00:23:01.050
And you may remember the disputes about the
Kaczynski twins and their sort of statements

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:04.560
about Europe and others.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:09.660
So that kind of politics could emerge as dominant
in Poland again.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:14.390
My personal view on it, though, is that it's
probably not going to have such a huge effect

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:18.740
on the economy, which is why I remain optimistic.

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:24.760
Obviously, geopolitical turmoil, you know,
that -- unresolved in Ukraine or, you know,

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:31.890
the relations with Russia are, you know, potentially
issues.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:36.660
But I guess those are really the main potential
blockages, that there's something that takes

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:40.690
it off this train that's zooming towards Europe.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:46.130
ROSE: Steve, a couple of years ago, Turkey
would have been a no-brainer for something

00:23:46.130 --> 00:23:47.130
like this.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.000
In fact, it was -- there were people who suggested
adding it to the BRICs, or various kinds of

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:55.850
-- you know, almost moving it up into the
super-stratosphere in developing economies.

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:58.920
It's cooled off a little bit, and there's
political turmoil.

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:03.640
So what are the -- why did Turkey do so well?

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:05.320
And where is it going now?

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:06.320
And what are the challenges?

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:07.850
COOK: Thanks, Gideon.

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:12.910
And thanks for including me in this, because
I did not contribute to this package, but

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.480
have been thinking and writing about Turkey,
including for Foreign Affairs, for a while.

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:17.480
I also want to thank you.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.580
For the last 10 minutes, I'm sorry, I didn't
pay any attention.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:22.960
I've been replaying my bar mitzvah with Psy
in it.

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:25.310
Totally the oddest thing I could -- anyway...

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:29.970
ROSE: Other ones this year have include Lorde
and Imagine Dragons, all on the Instagram

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:30.970
circuit.

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:31.970
It's really ridiculous.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:36.360
COOK: In any event, look, you know, Turkey
-- and you're absolutely right.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.590
In the last decade, Turkey certainly would
have been a no-brainer.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:43.110
And I think a lot of people tried to work
the T into the BRICs.

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:48.950
And the Turks certainly wanted the T in there
and often talked about how they should be

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.710
included among those countries.

00:24:50.710 --> 00:24:58.430
And I think that the genesis of the kind of
great 2000s Turkish economic boom goes back

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:05.040
to the economic crisis of 2000 and 2001, when
a government prior to the Justice and Development

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:14.720
Party, before Tayyip Erdogan came to power,
undertook some very serious and painful reforms.

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:20.970
Those reforms are authored by Kemal Dervis,
our colleague from the Brookings Institution,

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:25.930
and despite some expectations, after the Justice
and Development Party came to power in 2002

00:25:25.930 --> 00:25:34.090
that they would pursue a populist economic
policies and not stick to the very kind of

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:40.120
stringent conditions that the IMF had imposed,
they were basically responsible stewards of

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:41.120
the economy.

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:46.940
So take that, they were able to instill confidence
in investors by sticking to an IMF program,

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:55.740
add it to a large, young population, a country
that does have a manufacturing base.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:26:01.381
Talking about automotive manufacturing, if
you're going to buy a Ford in Europe, it was

00:26:01.381 --> 00:26:04.010
manufactured in Turkey.

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:08.360
Then you have location.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:14.440
Turkey sits at an important location, access
both Central Asia, Eurasia, the Middle East,

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:19.710
Europe, the Balkans, the Eastern Mediterranean,
extraordinarily important.

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:25.070
And over the course of this decade, you had
the rise of new Turkish entrepreneurs, these

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:29.770
kind of fearless entrepreneurs which show
up everywhere around the world wanting to

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:30.770
trade.

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:35.240
I remember just by an anecdote, I was having
dinner with one of these representatives from

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:38.210
one of the Turkish trade associations.

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:43.190
And he was aware of my interest in Egypt and
Egyptian politics and Egyptian economy.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:45.450
And he said, oh, we have a very small investment
in Egypt.

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:46.450
I said, oh, really?

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:47.450
You know, how many?

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:50.030
He said, oh, well, we employ 70,000 people
in Egypt.

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:53.360
I thought that was pretty extraordinary.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:27:00.680
So you have the combination of, I think, good
economic stewardship, a young and growing

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:07.030
population, a manufacturing base, to some
extent, good location, and then this kind

00:27:07.030 --> 00:27:13.110
of bubble about, you know, Turkey as a liberalizing,
democratizing country.

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:18.260
I mean, one of the advantages that Turkey
really had in the 2000s was that it wasn't

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:22.850
Turkey in the 1990s, where you had a series
of unstable governments that didn't make sense.

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:24.960
You had a coup d'etat.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:28.580
The Turks, you know, kind of bristle at the
idea that it was a coup, but they call it

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.030
the blanc (ph) coup, the postmodern coup.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:33.870
The fact of the matter is, is that the military
pushed a government out of power, produced

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:36.230
a significant amount of political instability.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:42.809
Tayyip Erdogan and AKP come to power in 2002
and 2003.

00:27:42.809 --> 00:27:46.710
And you suddenly don't have the need for coalition
governments.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:48.790
You have a strong leader.

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:54.690
And the AKP, different from previous governments,
looked out to the world.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:59.060
This was the party, this was the government
that was going to bring Turkey to the world.

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:05.970
Turkey historically had been very insular,
inward-looking, a kind of prickly nationalism.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:11.390
Foreign investment was there, but not in the
kind of way then when the AKP opened up the

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:12.390
country.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:17.930
I think the reason why Turkey has cooled off
is because, despite all the great promise

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:25.800
and these factors that have led to -- that
led to the great, you know, boom of the 2000s,

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:31.490
is that, one, the country's largely dependent
on financing a current account deficit through

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:32.490
foreign investment.

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:34.290
And that's essentially hot money.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:41.380
Turkey has no energy resources to speak of
and must import all of its gas, and the only

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:44.770
way to finance that is through foreign investment.

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:50.840
Current account deficit is a structural problem.

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:56.900
Over the course of the decade, as Turks became
or believed that they were wealthier, you

00:28:56.900 --> 00:29:01.940
saw -- again, nobody really wanted to talk
about it, but a consumer credit bubble emerging.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:07.450
You know, I'd go back every couple months,
and you'd see a neighborhood transformed.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:12.290
You'd see people who -- you know, friends
who told me that, you know, the guy -- the

00:29:12.290 --> 00:29:16.610
neighbor didn't have a BMW two months ago,
now had a BMW.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:21.020
There was clearly -- Turks were feeling wealthier
and starting to use credit when credit was

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:24.140
unavailable to them before.

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:32.680
You also had a very significant real estate
bubble.

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:38.700
I mean, if you look at the skyline of Istanbul,
it's been absolutely transformed over the

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:40.230
course of the last decade.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:41.309
Some of that's real.

00:29:41.309 --> 00:29:47.450
A lot of that has to do with crony capitalism
in Erdogan's Turkey and that the construction

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:51.390
industry has essentially become a vehicle
for patronage.

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:58.580
Add to that an illiberal turn in politics
that kind of exploded before everybody's eyes

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:01.590
during the Gezi Park protests this summer.

00:30:01.590 --> 00:30:06.490
Add to that the corruption scandal that's
been rocking the country over the course of

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:13.150
the last month and you have the problems that
Turkey is confronting now, and along with

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:18.970
changes in -- or anticipated changes in Fed
policies, growth in the United States, people

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:22.570
are pulling their money out of Turkey as quickly
as possible.

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:26.120
And the lira took a massive nose dive.

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:32.550
The central bank last night, in an emergency
meeting, hiked the interest rates, seemed

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:36.650
to have stabilized the situation, and it was
something very, very important.

00:30:36.650 --> 00:30:40.760
I wrote about this for FA.com a couple weeks
ago.

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:42.250
Something very important happened.

00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:45.050
Erdogan had lobbied against this interest
rate hike.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:51.330
He has been laying the groundwork for his
survival in this very difficult economic environment

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:55.940
since the summer by talking about the interest
rate lobby, the international bankers, Zionists,

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:56.940
Islamophobes.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:02.700
I've been called every single one of these
things, by the way, by any number of Turks.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:12.870
And he lobbied against it, but he said ultimately
he had no power to prevent it from happening.

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:20.940
To me, that was an -- that's a double-edged
sword, because I think in -- I think what

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:28.040
prevented, what put the break on Erdogan really
putting the screws to the central banker,

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:33.130
Erdem Basci, who, you know, many people were
holding out a hero for defying Erdogan, but

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:39.460
who previously had been seen as kind of a
-- you know, too close to the prime minister.

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:45.200
I think had it not been this massive fear
of what might happen had they not hiked interest

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.820
rates, Erdogan might have gotten his way.

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:51.230
And I think that what that speaks to -- and
I think this is a fundamental weakness in

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:58.429
Turkey -- is perverse institutions, either
weak or perverse institutions, in which at

00:31:58.429 --> 00:32:04.710
crisis moments, political leaders either use
them for their own non-democratic agendas

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:06.220
or go around them.

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:11.370
And I think that, had there not been this
massive, massive fear about what might happen

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:15.600
had there not been an interest rate hike,
it would have been business as usual and Erdogan

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:18.990
would have applied as much pressure on the
central bank as possible not to have the interest

00:32:18.990 --> 00:32:19.990
rate hike.

00:32:19.990 --> 00:32:20.990
ROSE: So are you completely bearish?

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:22.520
Or is there any bullish aspect to it?

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:27.110
COOK: You know, it's hard to see where -- you
know, there are -- you know, there have been

00:32:27.110 --> 00:32:31.280
some, you know, positive -- there was positive
investment in real things in Turkey.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:36.140
There was a recent big investment in the dairy
industry, which seems like the right thing,

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:42.500
but a lot of the investment has not been towards
productive, real kind of investment.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:47.570
I think that, you know, when Turkey comes
out on the other end, there has -- you will

00:32:47.570 --> 00:32:50.720
see that, you know, the AKP record is not
all bad.

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:59.320
There's been significant improvement in infrastructure,
airports built, high-speed railways, highways,

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:00.500
and so on and so forth.

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:08.620
But basically, this economy has been, you
know, jacked up by fiscal policy and hot money

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:14.250
and reputation for a government that's stable,
and suddenly it doesn't look as stable as

00:33:14.250 --> 00:33:15.250
it once was.

00:33:15.250 --> 00:33:19.720
I'm not talking about, you know, Egypt instability,
but we're talking about a significant amount

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:24.370
of political ferment for the foreseeable future,
and I think that's what the markets are responding

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:25.370
to.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:27.040
I don't see a way out.

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:30.870
Nobody's going to give up in this struggle
in Turkey, and I think it will -- there will

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:32.679
be iterations of this ongoing.

00:33:32.679 --> 00:33:34.090
ROSE: It's funny.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:37.590
You talked about the acronym and trying to
get Turkey into the BRICs.

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:43.180
When we were putting together the package,
we thought about an acronym, and I actually

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:47.500
came up with one that was our temporary internal
name for the package, which was the GEMs,

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:48.970
the growing emerging markets.

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:55.090
And my staff ultimately vetoed that and said
the acronyms are stupid, don't do acronyms,

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:57.940
and so forth and so -- so, fine, we didn't.

00:33:57.940 --> 00:33:58.940
OK.

00:33:58.940 --> 00:34:00.350
Well, this has been great.

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:07.060
Let me take one question from the chair before
I turn it over to our members and participants.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:16.889
If we had Shannon here, if we had Karen Brooks
here, who talked about, you know, the Philippines,

00:34:16.889 --> 00:34:22.779
we talked about Mexico, one of the questions
we'd be asking is about the role of leadership.

00:34:22.779 --> 00:34:29.809
So you have Aquino in the Philippines or Pena
Nieto in Mexico who managed to turn their

00:34:29.809 --> 00:34:34.749
countries around and pull off reforms that
their predecessors hadn't either been interested

00:34:34.749 --> 00:34:37.149
in or able to.

00:34:37.149 --> 00:34:40.030
Have leadership -- and I see that -- to a
certain extent, we see this in Turkey with

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:44.849
Erdogan playing a negative role, leadership
being important there, not just on the upside,

00:34:44.849 --> 00:34:47.659
but then screwing it up.

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:54.789
In Poland and in South Korea, and to one extent
in Turkey, too, how much of a crucial variable

00:34:54.789 --> 00:34:58.660
has personal leadership been in driving successes?

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:06.410
NOLAND: Well, in the case of South Korea,
South Korea has a form of government where

00:35:06.410 --> 00:35:12.480
the president is quite strong, and so the
quality of leadership has made a big difference.

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:21.000
But I think it -- and I think that people
probably underestimated President Park Geun-hye,

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:24.799
including the North Koreans, and I think probably
part of this was related to the fact she's

00:35:24.799 --> 00:35:26.220
a woman.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:32.249
And she's turned out to be a tougher, kind
of more committed, harder, you know -- she's

00:35:32.249 --> 00:35:36.470
a more forceful and stronger leader than I
think some people expected.

00:35:36.470 --> 00:35:40.930
But I think another aspect of it -- and I
was really reminded of this listening to Steve

00:35:40.930 --> 00:35:49.660
-- if you compare South Korea and Turkey right
now, there are -- there are two big differences,

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:54.920
the reason why Turkey's gotten hit with this
financial -- emerging financial crisis and

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.200
South Korea really hasn't.

00:35:56.200 --> 00:36:02.540
First of all, South Korea is running a trade
surplus; Turkey's running a deficit.

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:11.390
But the more important point is that Turkey
-- a lot of that deficit is -- what you had

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:18.690
in Turkey was you had Turkish banks borrowing
from European banks in euros short and lending

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:23.260
to construction firms in Anatolia and so forth
in lira long.

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:27.490
And that's created both term and currency
mismatch.

00:36:27.490 --> 00:36:28.690
And that's very dangerous.

00:36:28.690 --> 00:36:33.930
And a lot of people initially were concerned
that the problem would emerge in Europe, that

00:36:33.930 --> 00:36:38.320
if something happened in Europe and the European
banking system contracted, they'd cut the

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:40.680
Turks off and Turks would be in trouble.

00:36:40.680 --> 00:36:44.740
It hasn't worked out that way, but that created
a dangerous situation.

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:49.220
In South Korea, South Korea got into some
trouble in the global financial crisis.

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:51.670
The won fell by 45 percent.

00:36:51.670 --> 00:36:53.460
They were subject to a sudden stop.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:58.330
And one of the things the South Korean financial
regulators recognized was that South Korean

00:36:58.330 --> 00:37:04.819
banks and South Korean firms were doing things
to indirectly create the same sorts of mismatches,

00:37:04.819 --> 00:37:08.140
and they stepped in and put a stop to it.

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:13.460
And so the point I would emphasize is, the
thing about South Korea, South Korea, like

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.759
any other country in the world, has the capacity
to screw things up.

00:37:16.759 --> 00:37:19.970
But one of the things that's really striking
about South Korea is they learn from their

00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:20.970
mistakes.

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:25.519
And -- and you have not only the quality of
leadership at the presidency level, but you

00:37:25.519 --> 00:37:31.460
have a quality of leadership in governing
institutions more broadly that they change,

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:36.670
they adjust, and so the financial regulatory
authorities in Korea now act with greater

00:37:36.670 --> 00:37:42.069
prudential activism than they did several
years ago.

00:37:42.069 --> 00:37:47.130
And that's -- it's helped them in this emerging
situation the last couple of weeks.

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:50.700
ROSE: Mitch, has leadership played a particularly
significant role in Poland?

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:55.140
ORENSTEIN: Yeah, I mean, this is a great question,
a very controversial one with regard to Poland.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:38:01.410
There's a number of leaders, economic leaders
who claim all the credit for Poland's success.

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:04.299
And I tend to be rather skeptical of those
claims.

00:38:04.299 --> 00:38:07.560
But one wouldn't want to say that leadership's
unimportant.

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:11.940
I think like in South Korea, where you're
talking about leadership goes very deep, right,

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:15.150
it's not only at the top level, but we're
talking about the bureaucracies, we're talking

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:16.970
about multiple decision-makers.

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:21.299
Poland's a country that's gone through -- I
can't even count the number of governments

00:38:21.299 --> 00:38:22.299
since 1989.

00:38:22.299 --> 00:38:27.849
I mean, I think it's like dozens, you know,
or at least over a dozen, anyway.

00:38:27.849 --> 00:38:31.739
It's a government where -- it's a country
where the reformist government that was put

00:38:31.739 --> 00:38:38.450
in, in 1990 lasted only about a year and then
was succeeded by a very populist right-wing

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:39.450
government.

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:40.720
It was succeeded by a left-wing government.

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:44.470
It was succeeded by another liberal government,
by another populist government, and it keeps

00:38:44.470 --> 00:38:45.730
on going.

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:47.150
A lot of political change.

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:52.980
So what's interesting about Poland's transformation
is that it's been carried forward by the entire

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:54.430
political class.

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:57.900
This -- remember, this happened -- this sort
of thing used to happen in the United States,

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:02.960
too, right, where there was like a bipartisan
sort of, you know, agenda going forward?

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:04.480
Well, Poland has that.

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:06.319
And what I would call that is not leadership.

00:39:06.319 --> 00:39:08.470
I would call that social consensus.

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:12.740
I would call that maybe an elite consensus
that there's only one direction to be going,

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:14.299
and that direction is towards the West.

00:39:14.299 --> 00:39:18.489
Whether you like it or not, whether you're
comfortable or not with all aspects of it,

00:39:18.489 --> 00:39:23.690
it's been widely recognized in Poland that
that is the game in town and that you'd better

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:25.119
play it.

00:39:25.119 --> 00:39:29.999
And I think that makes it -- that's why I
talk about this in a more geopolitical space.

00:39:29.999 --> 00:39:31.440
Why did that consensus emerge?

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:35.150
I think that's the thing that's been missing
throughout Polish history.

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:37.619
Poland's famous for what's called the liberum
veto.

00:39:37.619 --> 00:39:45.630
Back in the 1500s, Poland was organized in
a quasi-democratic arrangement of nobility,

00:39:45.630 --> 00:39:51.700
but any one person could veto whatever the
policy was of the day, and that was what ultimately

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:54.030
led to the demise of Poland as a state.

00:39:54.030 --> 00:40:00.039
How did this country develop such a strong
consensus on Westernization, where its neighbors,

00:40:00.039 --> 00:40:04.329
its near neighbors, Ukraine has not, Belarus
has not?

00:40:04.329 --> 00:40:09.700
And I think it had to do with a specific historical
juncture they were at and a sense among the

00:40:09.700 --> 00:40:16.210
broad elite that they couldn't mess this up,
it was a unique opportunity to get to the

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:17.960
West, and they were going to do that.

00:40:17.960 --> 00:40:23.670
So I think that one should look skeptically
on claims of particular individual leaders

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:25.999
who claim a great deal of credit for this.

00:40:25.999 --> 00:40:31.119
I see many, many people there who can claim
a lot of credit.

00:40:31.119 --> 00:40:33.089
ROSE: Steve, a few words about leadership
in Turkey, pro and con?

00:40:33.089 --> 00:40:38.080
COOK: You know, I would -- I would agree with,
you know, Mitchell's last point that, you

00:40:38.080 --> 00:40:41.890
know, you have to be skeptical of these claims
about a single leader.

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:47.510
After all, as I said, many of the reforms
that put Turkey on the path of growth in 2000

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:51.579
were put in place by Kemal Dervis and a previous
government.

00:40:51.579 --> 00:40:54.380
But there is something about Tayyip Erdogan.

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:58.380
First of all, he is the best politician I
have ever seen this side of Bill Clinton.

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:04.739
He just has this innate ability to connect
with Turks and understands what Turks -- what

00:41:04.739 --> 00:41:06.720
makes Turks tick.

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:09.269
And it is absolutely magnetic.

00:41:09.269 --> 00:41:16.430
And, you know, it's -- it took that kind of
leadership, that kind of political skill,

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:24.309
I think, to undertake some of the really difficult
reforms that, particularly in the political

00:41:24.309 --> 00:41:26.880
sphere, that led to this decade of stability.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:30.530
It took Tayyip Erdogan to bring the military
to heel.

00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:37.720
Now, it hasn't ended up as well as it might
have, but it took this type of personality

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:43.279
to kind of wrench Turkey out of that malaise
and that instability of the 1990s.

00:41:43.279 --> 00:41:47.660
The question is, now has the time passed for
Erdogan?

00:41:47.660 --> 00:41:54.369
Because he now is creating his own distortions,
because of a certain arrogance of power, because

00:41:54.369 --> 00:42:00.570
of concern -- starting back as far as 2007,
concern that the system was going to strike

00:42:00.570 --> 00:42:01.570
back.

00:42:01.570 --> 00:42:06.029
He has resorted to a series of authoritarian
measures to establish control and institutionalize

00:42:06.029 --> 00:42:07.710
the power of the party.

00:42:07.710 --> 00:42:12.019
That has now clearly caught up with them,
and that's why the markets are pricing all

00:42:12.019 --> 00:42:15.019
of this risk into Turkey.

00:42:15.019 --> 00:42:22.420
So he may have been appropriate, he may have
been necessary in the early 2000s and through

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:26.009
the first decade of the 21st century, but
the question is, is he the appropriate leader

00:42:26.009 --> 00:42:27.799
to bring Turkey?

00:42:27.799 --> 00:42:28.799
I don't know.

00:42:28.799 --> 00:42:32.319
But, again, I'm skeptical of these, you know,
great man kind of arguments.

00:42:32.319 --> 00:42:33.319
ROSE: OK.

00:42:33.319 --> 00:42:35.569
At this point, let's bring all of you into
the conversation.

00:42:35.569 --> 00:42:40.500
So stand, state your name after I call on
you, and wait for the microphone.

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:49.359
This is on-the-record, unlike usual Council
sessions, so let's start out over here.

00:42:49.359 --> 00:42:51.559
Yes?

00:42:51.559 --> 00:42:55.829
QUESTION: I'm Glen Fukushima with the Center
for American Progress.

00:42:55.829 --> 00:42:58.819
I have a question for my friend, Marcus Noland.

00:42:58.819 --> 00:43:02.119
Before I put my money into South Korea, I
have two questions.

00:43:02.119 --> 00:43:07.450
Number one, is the contingent liability you
talked about, North Korea -- how would you

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:14.539
assess the impact of unification, say, over
the next 5 or 10 years with the case of Germany?

00:43:14.539 --> 00:43:18.900
And what impact do you think that unification
will have on the South Korean economy, in

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:20.349
terms of investment?

00:43:20.349 --> 00:43:23.819
And, second -- the second question is, when
I was in Davos at the World Economic Forum

00:43:23.819 --> 00:43:28.340
last week, a number of the South Koreans there
expressed some real concern about the fact

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:38.170
that Korea is caught among the trilateral
giants of China, Japan and the United States.

00:43:38.170 --> 00:43:42.390
The relationship with the United States is
going very well, but with Japan, with the

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:47.579
comfort women issue, with the Yasukuni Shrine,
with Dokdo Island, Takeshima Island, all these

00:43:47.579 --> 00:43:50.499
history issues and territorial issues, that's
a real problem.

00:43:50.499 --> 00:43:55.130
With China, there's closer economic relations,
but politically there's not necessarily consistency.

00:43:55.130 --> 00:44:00.819
So how do you see South Korea navigating among
these three giants?

00:44:00.819 --> 00:44:04.099
NOLAND: So how long are we going to go?

00:44:04.099 --> 00:44:05.099
Two hours?

00:44:05.099 --> 00:44:12.539
OK, on the first question, Korean unification
would -- OK, assuming it all went smoothly,

00:44:12.539 --> 00:44:17.410
didn't involve detonation of nuclear devices
or anything like that, if you just -- a very

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:26.079
nice, comfortable, you know, East German-style
collapse, right, no mass violence, the Korean

00:44:26.079 --> 00:44:29.720
case would be much bigger and more daunting
that the German case.

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:34.859
North Korea is much larger relative to South
Korea than East Germany was to West Germany.

00:44:34.859 --> 00:44:40.289
It is my poorer relative to South Korea than
East Germany was relative to West Germany.

00:44:40.289 --> 00:44:46.970
It's probably a more screwed up, distorted
economy and has a -- you know, all due respect

00:44:46.970 --> 00:44:51.809
to the Stasi, even a more screwed-up political
culture than East Germany.

00:44:51.809 --> 00:44:54.640
So this will be a big deal.

00:44:54.640 --> 00:45:01.319
People like me who model this, we need some
sort of end point to kind of anchor our models.

00:45:01.319 --> 00:45:07.839
So a typical way of modeling this is to say,
how much money would it take to raise North

00:45:07.839 --> 00:45:13.950
Korean incomes to 60 percent of those of the
South, 60 percent being rough difference between

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:18.289
the poorest and richest U.S. state, rough
difference between the poorest and richest

00:45:18.289 --> 00:45:23.260
South Korean province, until recently, roughly
different between the richest and poorest

00:45:23.260 --> 00:45:24.729
parts of the E.U.?

00:45:24.729 --> 00:45:30.640
And it turns out that -- you know, that depends
partly on how fast you can absorb new technology

00:45:30.640 --> 00:45:34.130
and how many people migrate across the border
and so on and so forth.

00:45:34.130 --> 00:45:39.269
But a rough ballpark estimate, plausible set
of parameters, it's going to cost more than

00:45:39.269 --> 00:45:42.980
$1 trillion over, say, a 10-year period.

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:47.829
And that means that this transitional period
is going to be long, you're going to have

00:45:47.829 --> 00:45:51.989
to do something about population influx control,
you're going to have to do something about

00:45:51.989 --> 00:45:55.630
voting rights for the current residents of
North Korea, how you integrate them into the

00:45:55.630 --> 00:45:57.119
political system.

00:45:57.119 --> 00:46:00.359
So it's not going to be a walk in the park.

00:46:00.359 --> 00:46:05.460
It could be really, really good for South
Korean construction companies, right?

00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:08.710
There's going to be a lot of work.

00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:13.859
Depending on how much of the money comes in
from abroad and how the exchange rate behaves,

00:46:13.859 --> 00:46:17.569
it may not be so good for South Korean manufacturing
exercises.

00:46:17.569 --> 00:46:24.680
So the bottom line is, if you're a South Korean
construction magnate with money to, you know,

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:29.510
invest in unification bonds, unification could
be very, very good for you.

00:46:29.510 --> 00:46:36.220
If you're a blue-collar worker in a traded
goods sector, you know, manufacturing something

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:41.809
for export, and now you suddenly have to compete
against your cousins up north, absent some

00:46:41.809 --> 00:46:46.430
compensatory policies, that could be a pretty
tough thing, leading to increased wealth and

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:48.490
income inequality.

00:46:48.490 --> 00:46:57.280
The geopolitical point, yeah, I mean, South
Korea has a tough situation.

00:46:57.280 --> 00:47:02.349
Right now, as you know, the relations with
Japan are fairly acrimonious.

00:47:02.349 --> 00:47:10.589
What's interesting about South Korea in the
economic sphere, at least, is that the South

00:47:10.589 --> 00:47:18.680
Koreans are using the kind of acrimony against
Japan to spur their own internal reforms.

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:24.099
I mean, high-level people, the finance minister,
the head of Korea development, have got up

00:47:24.099 --> 00:47:29.549
in public and said things like, you know,
Japan's gone through two decades of nothing,

00:47:29.549 --> 00:47:33.840
the third arrow of Abenomics is going to fail,
we don't want to do that, so we've got to

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:37.969
start structural reforms today, right now,
seriously.

00:47:37.969 --> 00:47:40.849
So they use their rivalry with Japan as a
spur.

00:47:40.849 --> 00:47:46.069
And the fact that it's gotten a little more
heated recently just means it's a better spur.

00:47:46.069 --> 00:47:48.359
The other thing that's curious is this.

00:47:48.359 --> 00:47:53.849
South -- the yen -- the Japanese yen has really
depreciated against the South Korean won.

00:47:53.849 --> 00:47:57.489
And that's hurt South Korean manufacturers
who compete against the Japanese directly

00:47:57.489 --> 00:47:58.930
or indirectly.

00:47:58.930 --> 00:48:05.099
I've been struck by the Park government has
not ridden the yen down, that they have said,

00:48:05.099 --> 00:48:09.160
no, we are going to do restructuring, we are
going to promote the service sector and creative

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:14.210
economy, we are going to shift demand towards
domestic demand and not rely so much on export

00:48:14.210 --> 00:48:15.210
markets.

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:19.119
And thus far -- this may not -- if we have
this meeting in a year, I may not be able

00:48:19.119 --> 00:48:25.160
to say this -- but thus far, they have abjured
the kind of quick fix of letting the won go

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:26.160
down.

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:29.529
Now, I know that doesn't get into all the
geopolitical issues you represented, but we've

00:48:29.529 --> 00:48:31.549
got two other people here, and they want to
talk, too.

00:48:31.549 --> 00:48:32.549
ROSE: Great, thank you.

00:48:32.549 --> 00:48:38.579
I would say that we actually have a piece
on Korean unification in the May-June issue

00:48:38.579 --> 00:48:43.750
coming up that will be a look at sort of how
it might play out, what its effects might

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:47.920
be, and so forth, so look for that one.

00:48:47.920 --> 00:48:51.019
Yes, over here.

00:48:51.019 --> 00:48:55.140
QUESTION: I'm Mitzi Wertheim with the Naval
Postgraduate School.

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:58.069
I want you to look into the future.

00:48:58.069 --> 00:49:05.140
How do you think 3-D manufacturing is going
to affect all of these economies?

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:09.750
Frankly, it scares the devil out of me, but
anyway.

00:49:09.750 --> 00:49:17.759
I mean, I go to these meetings and listen
to mercs talk about they won't have any jobs

00:49:17.759 --> 00:49:23.690
because all the 3-D creation will be done
at home and we won't have to transport anything.

00:49:23.690 --> 00:49:30.049
ROSE: Are any of you up on digital fabrication
and could you speak authoritatively to that

00:49:30.049 --> 00:49:31.049
point?

00:49:31.049 --> 00:49:33.839
NOLAND: I think it's like Clint Eastwood said
in one of those movies.

00:49:33.839 --> 00:49:37.309
A man's got to know his limitations.

00:49:37.309 --> 00:49:38.309
(LAUGHTER)

00:49:38.309 --> 00:49:39.789
ROSE: You know, it's interesting...

00:49:39.789 --> 00:49:40.789
ORENSTEIN: I think you exceeded mine.

00:49:40.789 --> 00:49:44.190
ROSE: The next issue has a lot of stuff on
tech in it, including stuff on the Internet

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:48.700
of things, by -- one of the co-authors is
Neil Gershenfeld, who wrote about digital

00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:49.700
fabrication.

00:49:49.700 --> 00:49:51.839
And this was actually a subject at Davos that
was talked about a lot.

00:49:51.839 --> 00:49:57.130
And the question about employment and machines
and are we in a second machine age, and if

00:49:57.130 --> 00:49:58.999
so, what's the future, it's actually fascinating.

00:49:58.999 --> 00:50:04.130
One of the most interesting discussions that
I heard at Davos was an off-site dinner in

00:50:04.130 --> 00:50:12.509
which Fareed Zakaria and Martin Wolf nearly
came to blows about whether the -- whether

00:50:12.509 --> 00:50:20.039
the U.S. slowness in employment, in terms
of the rebound, after the economy rebounded,

00:50:20.039 --> 00:50:25.239
was a harbinger of a fundamentally new situation
in which there's going to be a lower employment

00:50:25.239 --> 00:50:31.509
plateau and so forth, or whether it was just
lack of demand and whether the transition

00:50:31.509 --> 00:50:34.970
to a new kind of economy will be like old
ones.

00:50:34.970 --> 00:50:37.170
We're going to cover this kind of issue more.

00:50:37.170 --> 00:50:38.180
Nobody actually -- Mitch, do you want to talk
about that one?

00:50:38.180 --> 00:50:40.119
ORENSTEIN: I'll just say a couple words.

00:50:40.119 --> 00:50:45.009
I mean, I can't -- I can't see it having an
enormous impact in Poland.

00:50:45.009 --> 00:50:49.969
If you can figure out how to print a Volkswagen
Golf, then, you know, I guess I would be wrong

00:50:49.969 --> 00:50:50.969
about that.

00:50:50.969 --> 00:50:53.490
But I think we're probably a long ways away
from that.

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:57.450
I don't see us being able to print washing
machines.

00:50:57.450 --> 00:51:01.390
And I neglected to mention, also, you know,
a lot of the industry, a lot of what's happening

00:51:01.390 --> 00:51:07.609
there is, you know, call centers or back-office
support operations.

00:51:07.609 --> 00:51:14.079
So my guess is that that won't necessarily
have an outsized impact on Poland.

00:51:14.079 --> 00:51:15.640
It's going to be...

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:16.969
QUESTION: Near term.

00:51:16.969 --> 00:51:19.730
ORENSTEIN: Yeah, near term, right, right,
as far as I can see, you know.

00:51:19.730 --> 00:51:23.719
COOK: I'd say the same thing with regard to
Turkey, not knowing really anything about

00:51:23.719 --> 00:51:28.170
digital fabrication, but I know two things,
one, the Turkish economy, you know, we're

00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:35.109
talking about big construction-type of things,
cars, you know, infrastructure projects, things

00:51:35.109 --> 00:51:36.109
like that.

00:51:36.109 --> 00:51:39.510
Until you can digitally fabricate an airport
runway, I thank the Turks are going to be

00:51:39.510 --> 00:51:41.499
OK.

00:51:41.499 --> 00:51:43.339
But I will say this.

00:51:43.339 --> 00:51:49.079
For a country of Turkey's size and levels
of education, they are extraordinarily late

00:51:49.079 --> 00:51:54.239
adopters when it comes to technology.

00:51:54.239 --> 00:51:59.219
You know, there is no design fabrication software
work really happening in Turkey.

00:51:59.219 --> 00:52:06.249
There is no kind of dynamic tech-type of sector
in Turkey at all.

00:52:06.249 --> 00:52:09.519
So over time, they may well be left behind.

00:52:09.519 --> 00:52:11.730
ROSE: Yes, over here.

00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:12.829
You two.

00:52:12.829 --> 00:52:14.200
Second row, then first row.

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:16.249
Yes, you first.

00:52:16.249 --> 00:52:22.619
QUESTION: Diana Lady Dougan, Center for Strategic
and International Studies.

00:52:22.619 --> 00:52:29.630
I wanted to pick on Steve for a moment because
of his expertise on both Egypt and Turkey.

00:52:29.630 --> 00:52:37.539
And I know we're talking about emerging economies
or upper-end economies that were developing

00:52:37.539 --> 00:52:46.180
countries, but the role of religion and the
military in both those countries, and -- and

00:52:46.180 --> 00:52:48.339
also in Korea, I might add.

00:52:48.339 --> 00:52:54.180
And by way of disclosure, I'm one of the producers
of a film called "The Square," and involved

00:52:54.180 --> 00:52:57.119
in also post-production.

00:52:57.119 --> 00:53:02.869
And I've been so struck by the demographics
of change.

00:53:02.869 --> 00:53:07.289
And when we're looking at the aging of some
of these countries -- and I think Turkey average

00:53:07.289 --> 00:53:14.970
age is about 29, and Egypt's is 25, but they
have a very highly educated workforce in Egypt,

00:53:14.970 --> 00:53:17.349
and there's a lot going on right now.

00:53:17.349 --> 00:53:22.479
But I guess I would like to, particularly
for you, but for others, to sort of extrapolate

00:53:22.479 --> 00:53:25.569
where demographics are going to come out in
this.

00:53:25.569 --> 00:53:31.930
And then, also, I can't help but think back
to some of your very articulate comments in

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:40.509
past years about how much the Egyptian military
is the capital of crony capitalism in Egypt

00:53:40.509 --> 00:53:42.789
and the degree to which this exists elsewhere.

00:53:42.789 --> 00:53:43.789
Thank you.

00:53:43.789 --> 00:53:45.660
COOK: Thank you for the question.

00:53:45.660 --> 00:53:48.264
I could spend the next three hours talking
about all of these things, so...

00:53:48.264 --> 00:53:49.264
ROSE: You don't have the next three hours.

00:53:49.264 --> 00:53:53.170
COOK: But I'm not going to -- I'm going to
get to the core of your question about demographics.

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:54.249
And it's funny.

00:53:54.249 --> 00:53:59.430
When you think about it, you know, for years,
people have been talking about these demographic

00:53:59.430 --> 00:54:04.999
issues, and it seemed like the dog that wouldn't
bark, but then suddenly it did bark.

00:54:04.999 --> 00:54:10.499
And I think that, looking at Egypt and then
looking at Turkey, and looking at the Gezi

00:54:10.499 --> 00:54:15.839
Park protests, and we cannot -- it was -- I
groan every time someone, you know, wanted

00:54:15.839 --> 00:54:20.700
to make the connection between Gezi, is this
the Turkish Spring, as Tahrir Square was -- became

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:22.880
representative of the Arab Spring.

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:29.569
But both are, you know, predominantly young,
educated, mobilized, but unassimilated into

00:54:29.569 --> 00:54:33.509
the political process type of movements.

00:54:33.509 --> 00:54:36.519
But I think that they're somewhat different.

00:54:36.519 --> 00:54:41.380
First, if there's anything that revolutionary
that has happened in Egypt over the course

00:54:41.380 --> 00:54:46.950
of the three years, and not much, actually,
revolutionary has happened, is the fact that

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:52.819
you do have this young demographic that is
making demands on the political system and

00:54:52.819 --> 00:54:58.910
articulating those demands in actually the
streets in a way that the authorities have

00:54:58.910 --> 00:55:04.750
no real answer for, other than to employ authoritarian
measures.

00:55:04.750 --> 00:55:11.099
That's not stable, because if their only answer
is to more violence, more force, and these

00:55:11.099 --> 00:55:17.380
people remain, you know, brave in the face
of it, it's a recipe for continued political

00:55:17.380 --> 00:55:18.529
instability.

00:55:18.529 --> 00:55:22.990
The same kind of thing happened in Gezi on
a smaller scale.

00:55:22.990 --> 00:55:29.319
Now, more and more people have come into the
Turkish political arena, but over time, over

00:55:29.319 --> 00:55:31.969
this decade, fewer and fewer have been able
to contest it.

00:55:31.969 --> 00:55:35.240
Now, with Gezi, it wasn't just an environmental
movement.

00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:38.750
It wasn't just about the redevelopment of
this park off Taksim Square.

00:55:38.750 --> 00:55:46.150
It was about police brutality, crony capitalism,
arrogance of power, the lack of access to

00:55:46.150 --> 00:55:51.109
the political arena, the inability to contest
it, the inability of political parties, other

00:55:51.109 --> 00:55:53.420
political parties to be effective.

00:55:53.420 --> 00:55:57.660
These are -- you know, this is the new reality
in Turkey, as well.

00:55:57.660 --> 00:56:03.880
The question is -- extrapolating now out -- in
both Egypt and Turkey is, how do these young,

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:08.589
mobilized people now translate that into the
formal political arena?

00:56:08.589 --> 00:56:13.880
In Egypt, large numbers of them absolutely
reject the idea of formal politics and want

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:15.369
to stay outside of it.

00:56:15.369 --> 00:56:17.329
Home is where the streets are.

00:56:17.329 --> 00:56:18.609
The streets are where home is.

00:56:18.609 --> 00:56:20.599
The street is where we've been most effective.

00:56:20.599 --> 00:56:23.049
We will only be co-opted if we go inside.

00:56:23.049 --> 00:56:25.670
In Turkey, they're still trying to figure
this out.

00:56:25.670 --> 00:56:30.950
They are -- many of them, these ineffective
political parties that exist are trying to

00:56:30.950 --> 00:56:32.289
co-opt them.

00:56:32.289 --> 00:56:40.209
Just by way of anecdote, I met a group of
them, wealthy, young, mobilized by the Gezi

00:56:40.209 --> 00:56:44.869
Park protests, at a meeting through mutual
friends over this past summer when I was there.

00:56:44.869 --> 00:56:48.339
And they said to me, "Oh, the leader of the
Republican People's Party, Kemal Kilicdaroglu,

00:56:48.339 --> 00:56:50.170
is going to be here in an hour."

00:56:50.170 --> 00:56:51.430
I said, "OK, what are your demands?

00:56:51.430 --> 00:56:52.430
Come on, quick.

00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:54.009
Let's write up what your demands are."

00:56:54.009 --> 00:56:58.809
It hadn't even dawned on them that they held
an upper hand.

00:56:58.809 --> 00:57:04.041
So Turkey, I think, that demographic is way
behind where the Egyptians are, and they're

00:57:04.041 --> 00:57:06.789
going to -- it's going to be a while until
they figure out the way forward.

00:57:06.789 --> 00:57:10.980
ROSE: Very quick comment on demographics in
Korea and Poland?

00:57:10.980 --> 00:57:14.609
NOLAND: Part of the Korean success story is
they've had the wind at their back.

00:57:14.609 --> 00:57:17.400
They've had very good demographics for the
last 50 years or so.

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:19.480
Now it's going into reverse.

00:57:19.480 --> 00:57:22.680
2010, the core productive population began
shrinking.

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:25.700
Next decade, dependency ratio goes up.

00:57:25.700 --> 00:57:30.971
And if you -- you know, if you extrapolate
out current trends, 2050, the population of

00:57:30.971 --> 00:57:35.309
the country absent Korean unification begins
falling.

00:57:35.309 --> 00:57:40.259
What it means for the government is, is that
old people consume more services and less

00:57:40.259 --> 00:57:43.099
manufacturers than younger people do.

00:57:43.099 --> 00:57:47.250
And a lot of -- and services, as I mentioned,
is where the economy really lags in terms

00:57:47.250 --> 00:57:48.250
of productivity.

00:57:48.250 --> 00:57:51.059
And a lot of services are provided by the
state.

00:57:51.059 --> 00:57:56.890
And so the Koreans understand they need to
get service productivity up and -- because

00:57:56.890 --> 00:58:01.709
that is not only going to, you know, help
them in terms of dealing with these demographic

00:58:01.709 --> 00:58:06.430
changes, but it also could be important in
terms of their fiscal position, as well.

00:58:06.430 --> 00:58:07.430
ROSE: Quick?

00:58:07.430 --> 00:58:11.140
ORENSTEIN: Yeah, on demographics in Poland,
a couple of points.

00:58:11.140 --> 00:58:16.329
One is that the Solidarity movement was consonant
with a big demographic boom in Poland.

00:58:16.329 --> 00:58:19.969
A lot of kids were born in the 1980s.

00:58:19.969 --> 00:58:25.000
And as a result, they're a relatively young
population in Europe, but that's not going

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:26.000
to last.

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:30.170
So they, too, are going to succumb to a lot
of the declines that Europe in general has

00:58:30.170 --> 00:58:31.820
been facing.

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:36.190
But that won't be until sort of 2030, approximately.

00:58:36.190 --> 00:58:40.160
The other -- sorry, one other point to point
out here is there's a huge out-migration of

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:41.960
Poles in the U.K., prominently...

00:58:41.960 --> 00:58:43.150
ROSE: All those Polish plumbers.

00:58:43.150 --> 00:58:44.309
COOK: The Polish plumbers.

00:58:44.309 --> 00:58:48.440
ORENSTEIN: Yes, primarily to the U.K., Ireland,
a couple other places.

00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:52.119
And one of the big internal issues is, are
you going to have those people come back at

00:58:52.119 --> 00:58:53.489
some point?

00:58:53.489 --> 00:58:57.069
And if the economy keeps growing, are you
going to end up with a labor shortage and

00:58:57.069 --> 00:59:00.200
to what extent immigration is going to occur
into Poland?

00:59:00.200 --> 00:59:03.460
And that's a very new issue, so I don't think
we can really say much about it.

00:59:03.460 --> 00:59:05.180
ROSE: Last quick question.

00:59:05.180 --> 00:59:09.119
Sorry for all those -- we could talk all night
on these things.

00:59:09.119 --> 00:59:12.119
QUESTION: Robert Warren (ph) from the Foreign
Service Institute.

00:59:12.119 --> 00:59:17.569
Dr. Noland, would you elaborate on points
you haven't made, really, and that is on the

00:59:17.569 --> 00:59:19.150
trade relationship.

00:59:19.150 --> 00:59:24.650
You have Korea now, I think, as partner in
the trade -- the Trans-Pacific Partnership.

00:59:24.650 --> 00:59:26.150
What's the meaning of this partnership?

00:59:26.150 --> 00:59:30.609
And how might it impact on us, as well as
Korea?

00:59:30.609 --> 00:59:36.190
NOLAND: We were just discussing this, you
know, before we came in here tonight.

00:59:36.190 --> 00:59:40.809
The basic problem is, is that trade policy
has become salient at the domestic level and

00:59:40.809 --> 00:59:43.220
it's become more pluralistic at the interstate
level.

00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:46.849
And so it's hard to get things done at the
global level.

00:59:46.849 --> 00:59:51.130
Politics abhors a vacuum, so these people
are following the path of least resistance,

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:55.240
which is global -- I mean, regional and plurilateral
agreements.

00:59:55.240 --> 00:59:56.730
In Asia, you basically have two.

00:59:56.730 --> 00:59:59.809
You have one in East Asia centered around
ASEAN.

00:59:59.809 --> 01:00:04.450
Then you have the Trans-Pacific Partnership,
which you mentioned.

01:00:04.450 --> 01:00:08.500
The issue with the Trans-Pacific Partnership
is most of those economies are pretty liberal

01:00:08.500 --> 01:00:10.650
already, and they already have free trade
agreements.

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:13.630
I mean, we already have a free trade agreement
with South Korea.

01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:20.670
So the marginal impact is not that great unless
you get other participants, such as Japan.

01:00:20.670 --> 01:00:26.330
But the dynamic impact is important, because
if Korea's in, Japan is going to want to be

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:27.330
in.

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:30.730
And eventually, you're going to end up with
a situation in which the United States and

01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:35.890
China are basically the only countries that
are not part of both of those blocks.

01:00:35.890 --> 01:00:41.539
So if the United States and China are smart,
we would be thinking about designing those

01:00:41.539 --> 01:00:47.200
agreements in a way that at the end of the
day they could be knitted together into one

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:48.460
big agreement.

01:00:48.460 --> 01:00:52.930
Or we're going to have a situation where all
the other countries in the Asia Pacific are

01:00:52.930 --> 01:00:57.140
freely trading with everyone else and then
we and the Chinese are the ones kind of, you

01:00:57.140 --> 01:01:00.430
know, stuck kind of staring at each other.

01:01:00.430 --> 01:01:02.759
ROSE: Any comments on trade in your countries?

01:01:02.759 --> 01:01:09.609
COOK: Yeah, I think that one of the advantages
that Turkey's had over the last decade was

01:01:09.609 --> 01:01:14.200
Turkey's rise as a trading state and the rise
of this entrepreneurial class that wanted

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:15.940
to trade with the world, in fact.

01:01:15.940 --> 01:01:21.290
These traders were kind of the leading edge
of a Turkish foreign policy that really spread

01:01:21.290 --> 01:01:24.859
its wings throughout the decade.

01:01:24.859 --> 01:01:30.369
The question is what kind of impact that will
have -- what kind of -- the current crisis

01:01:30.369 --> 01:01:35.099
in the political instability will have on
this -- on this class?

01:01:35.099 --> 01:01:36.700
Where will they go?

01:01:36.700 --> 01:01:42.700
These people were predominantly supporters
of the AKP.

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:50.000
This political crisis combined with this currency
crisis is just at the beginning.

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:52.550
I think it's unclear what's going to happen
to them.

01:01:52.550 --> 01:01:59.069
But at least they have the infrastructure
and large numbers of people who are trading,

01:01:59.069 --> 01:02:05.119
they have things to trade, they have advantages
in certain areas, that they could come out

01:02:05.119 --> 01:02:10.809
on the end with no trouble whatsoever, because
that's separate -- those advantages are separate

01:02:10.809 --> 01:02:16.180
from the political scandal that's happening
and the currency issues.

01:02:16.180 --> 01:02:21.000
ROSE: And, Mitch, is the -- is Poland's being
part of the E.U., but not part of the eurozone

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:22.150
the right switch, the sweet spot?

01:02:22.150 --> 01:02:24.140
ORENSTEIN: Oh, it certainly has been, yeah.

01:02:24.140 --> 01:02:29.589
I mean, I would say, obviously, Poland benefits
from being part of a big and functional regional

01:02:29.589 --> 01:02:33.469
free trade -- or regional trade agreement,
the European Union.

01:02:33.469 --> 01:02:36.529
That's obviously a huge advantage for the
economy.

01:02:36.529 --> 01:02:40.180
But I would say that -- I'm sort of the outlier
here, as we talked about earlier.

01:02:40.180 --> 01:02:44.230
I think we've seen a collapse of the global
free trade regime, or the beginnings of a

01:02:44.230 --> 01:02:48.259
collapse of the global free trade regime,
and perhaps that's because I'm looking at

01:02:48.259 --> 01:02:51.369
it from the vantage point of Europe and not
the vantage point of Asia.

01:02:51.369 --> 01:02:56.009
But what I see, in Poland's neck of the woods,
is that it's part of this big trade bloc called

01:02:56.009 --> 01:02:59.999
the E.U. and it's right next to another trade
bloc that's trying to be built called the

01:02:59.999 --> 01:03:01.180
Eurasian Union.

01:03:01.180 --> 01:03:06.299
Notice it has the same initials, too, you
know?

01:03:06.299 --> 01:03:08.700
And that's a real challenge for a place like
Poland.

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:13.829
When we see in that region of the world trade
barriers going up between those two -- now,

01:03:13.829 --> 01:03:18.109
those are natural trading partners for Poland,
so this is why -- you know, there's the -- it's

01:03:18.109 --> 01:03:25.339
a big deal for the E.U. to have Russia creating
a big alternative trading bloc that will have

01:03:25.339 --> 01:03:27.619
barriers against the E.U.

01:03:27.619 --> 01:03:34.609
So I think that Poland can react as it has
by being a Western-leaning state.

01:03:34.609 --> 01:03:39.619
Maybe it will accentuate that, because that's
where it can trade freely, but it's an opportunity

01:03:39.619 --> 01:03:40.619
cost.

01:03:40.619 --> 01:03:47.849
ROSE: In a piece accompanying the package,
Ruchir Sharma talks about how the signal mark

01:03:47.849 --> 01:03:52.049
going forward of the emerging markets will
be differentiation, that instead of thinking

01:03:52.049 --> 01:03:57.209
of the developing world as a giant class in
which it will rise or fall as a group, you

01:03:57.209 --> 01:04:02.440
have to look at the individual countries and
their particular circumstances and pick your

01:04:02.440 --> 01:04:03.609
bets very carefully.

01:04:03.609 --> 01:04:09.119
And I think that what we've heard tonight
confirms that, which is that each of these

01:04:09.119 --> 01:04:12.960
countries has very specific histories and
very specific problems and issues.

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:17.039
And while there are some similarities, you
need to have -- you need to drill down on

01:04:17.039 --> 01:04:19.680
the specifics of each country, with people
who know what they're talking about, and we've

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:22.880
been very fortunate, I think, to have access
to these people.

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:27.709
And I want to thank Marcus and Mitch and Steve
and thank all of you for coming.

01:04:27.709 --> 01:04:29.359
And until the next FA Live.

01:04:29.359 --> 01:04:29.509
(APPLAUSE)

